{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt && rg -n \\\"TopoPilot|topopilot|degener|trisector|wedge|render\\\" /workspace -g '*.py' -g '*.txt' -g '*.md' 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"TOPOPILOT SCRIPTING GUIDE\n=========================\n\nThis guide describes the current scripting API in TopoPilot2Library. The canonical\nstarting point is:\n\n    TopoPilot2Library/src/topopilot/ui/script_template.py\n\nCopy that file to a new Python script and edit the two placeholder functions:\n\n    def pipeline(load_path):\n        pass\n\n    def create_view():\n        pass\n\nThe template already imports ParaView and every public TopoPilot node, validates\ncommand-line arguments, creates the session/cache directories, renders an image or\ninteractive view, and renders time-varying data to frames before invoking ffmpeg.\n\n\n1. THE NODE PIPELINE MODEL\n==========================\n\nA TopoPilot pipeline is a directed tree of node objects (or a forest if it has\nmultiple independent loaders).\n\n  * A loader is a root node. It reads a .vti file or a directory containing a time\n    series.\n  * Every other node has exactly one parent, named by parent_node_id.\n  * A compute/operation node transforms its parent's output into another typed\n    result (for example, Scalar -> PersistenceDiagram).\n  * An Embed... node turns a computed result into displayable ParaView geometry.\n  * Only displayable embedding nodes should normally be placed in\n    nodes_to_visualize.\n\nConstruct a child after its parent and pass parent.id, not the parent object:\n\n    scalar = LoadScalarField(load_path=load_path, arrayName=\"pressure\")\n    simplified = PersistenceSimplification(\n        parent_node_id=scalar.id,\n        epsilon=0.05,\n    )\n    diagram = ComputePersistenceDiagram(parent_node_id=simplified.id)\n    diagram_view = EmbedPersistenceDiagram(parent_node_id=diagram.id)\n\nThe node tree checks that the parent's output type is compatible with the child.\nNodes are registered as they are constructed and receive a short string id. If an\nidentical root, or an identical child with the same parent and parameters, already\nexists in the in-memory node tree, TopoPilot reuses its id. Node definitions should\ntherefore remain inside pipeline(), where the template expects to create them.\n\nBranches are allowed. For example, the same scalar node may feed both a scalar\nfield embedding and a contour-tree computation:\n\n    scalar_view = EmbedScalarField(parent_node_id=scalar.id, colormap=\"viridis\")\n    tree = ComputeContourTree(parent_node_id=scalar.id)\n    tree_view = EmbedContourTree(parent_node_id=tree.id)\n\n\n2. EDITING pipeline(load_path)\n================================\n\npipeline() must build the nodes and return exactly four values:\n\n    def pipeline(load_path):\n        # 1. Create one loader/root.\n        scalar = LoadScalarField(load_path=load_path, arrayName=\"pressure\")\n\n        # 2. Chain computation nodes using parent.id.\n        simplified = PersistenceSimplification(\n            parent_node_id=scalar.id,\n            epsilon=0.05,\n        )\n        contours = ComputeIsocontours(\n            parent_node_id=simplified.id,\n            num_contours=12,\n        )\n\n        # 3. End displayed branches with embedding nodes.\n        field_view = EmbedScalarField(\n            parent_node_id=simplified.id,\n            colormap=\"cool warm\",\n        )\n        contour_view = EmbedIsocontours(\n            parent_node_id=contours.id,\n            colormap=\"black body\",\n            tube_radius=1.0,\n        )\n\n        # 4. Select the embeddings that the template will render.\n        nodes_to_visualize = [field_view, contour_view]\n\n        # Optional scalar-bar placement, keyed by an embedding node's id.\n        # Value: ([x, y], relative_length, title)\n        color_bar_info = {\n            field_view.id: ([0.82, 0.15], 0.70, \"pressure\"),\n        }\n\n        # These lists are emitted by the UI code generator as cache-planning\n        # metadata. Keep them in the return signature. In the current script\n        # template they are unpacked but are not otherwise consumed.\n        time_invariant_nodes_to_cache = []\n        normal_nodes_to_cache = []\n\n        return (\n            nodes_to_visualize,\n            color_bar_info,\n            time_invariant_nodes_to_cache,\n            normal_nodes_to_cache,\n        )\n\nImportant details:\n\n  * Always use the load_path argument supplied to pipeline(), rather than hard-coding\n    the input filename. The -i CLI option supplies this value.\n  * A single input is a .vti file.\n  * A time series is a directory named NAME containing NAME1.vti, NAME2.vti, ...,\n    with consecutive one-based numbering and no unrelated directory entries.\n  * Use the exact point-array names stored in the VTI file when constructing a\n    loader. The loaders rename them internally to TopoPilot's standard names.\n  * The nodes_to_visualize order is also the rendering order. Put a background\n    surface/volume before points, contours, or trees that should appear over it.\n  * color_bar_info is optional. Omit an entry for a node that needs no scalar bar.\n    Position uses normalized view coordinates; length is a normalized fraction.\n  * Scalar/contour colormap choices currently are: grey, eigenvector partition,\n    eigenvalue partition, bent cool warm, black body, cool warm, extended kindlmann,\n    fast, inferno, kindlmann, plasma, and viridis.\n\n\n3. EDITING create_view()\n========================\n\ncreate_view() must create and return a ParaView RenderView. Camera and viewport\nsettings belong here, not in pipeline():\n\n    def create_view():\n        view = CreateView(\"RenderView\")\n\n        view.ViewSize = [1280, 720]\n        view.OrientationAxesVisibility = 1\n        view.CenterOfRotation = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n        view.CameraPosition = [0.0, 0.0, 100.0]\n        view.CameraFocalPoint = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n        view.CameraViewUp = [0.0, 1.0, 0.0]\n        view.CameraParallelScale = 50.0\n        view.CameraParallelProjection = True\n        view.CameraViewAngle = 30.0\n        view.InteractionMode = \"2D\"\n\n        return view\n\nThe template sets a white background for a single time step after create_view()\nreturns. For video rendering, set any required background properties directly in\ncreate_view(), because the multi-frame branch does not apply that single-frame\nbackground override. Use even ViewSize dimensions for video encoding.\n\nThe TopoPilot UI can generate this function from its current ParaView camera. If\nwriting it by hand, adjust CameraPosition, CameraFocalPoint, CameraViewUp, and\nCameraParallelScale until the data is framed correctly. For a 3D view, use an\nappropriate camera and change InteractionMode (commonly to \"3D\").\n\n\n4. COMPLETE MINIMAL SCRIPT EDIT\n===============================\n\nStarting from script_template.py, the smallest useful replacements are:\n\n    def pipeline(load_path):\n        scalar = LoadScalarField(load_path=load_path, arrayName=\"pressure\")\n        field_view = EmbedScalarField(\n            parent_node_id=scalar.id,\n            colormap=\"viridis\",\n        )\n\n        nodes_to_visualize = [field_view]\n        color_bar_info = {\n            field_view.id: ([0.82, 0.15], 0.70, \"pressure\"),\n        }\n        return nodes_to_visualize, color_bar_info, [], []\n\n    def create_view():\n        view = CreateView(\"RenderView\")\n        view.ViewSize = [1280, 720]\n        view.CameraPosition = [0.0, 0.0, 100.0]\n        view.CameraFocalPoint = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n        view.CameraViewUp = [0.0, 1.0, 0.0]\n        view.CameraParallelProjection = True\n        view.CameraParallelScale = 50.0\n        view.InteractionMode = \"2D\"\n        return view\n\n\n5. RUNNING THE SCRIPT\n=====================\n\nRun it in an environment containing TopoPilot, ParaView, and TTK.\n\nSingle VTI to PNG:\n\n    pvbatch my_pipeline.py -i ./data.vti -t 1 -o output.png\n\nSingle VTI interactively:\n\n    python3 my_pipeline.py -i ./data.vti -t 1 -x\n\nAll steps of a time-series directory to MP4:\n\n    pvbatch my_pipeline.py -i path/to/NAME -t -1 -o output.mp4 -q session\n\nOne time step from a time-series directory to PNG:\n\n    pvbatch my_pipeline.py -i path/to/NAME -t 4 -o output.png -q session\n\nCLI options:\n\n  -i PATH   Required .vti file or time-series directory.\n  -x        Interactive mode; cannot be combined with -o, and cannot display a\n            multi-step input with -t -1.\n  -o PATH   Output .png for one step or .mp4 for all steps.\n  -t N      One-based time step; -1 means all steps (default).\n  -q DIR    Session, cache, and temporary-frame directory (default: current dir).\n\nVideo output requires ffmpeg on PATH. The template currently invokes it at 4 fps.\n\n\n6. NODE REFERENCE\n=================\n\nIn every non-loader constructor, parent_node_id is the id of the immediately\npreceding compatible node. Defaults are shown below.\n\n\nDATA LOADERS (ROOT NODES)\n-------------------------\n\nLoadScalarField(load_path, arrayName=\"\")\n    Loads a scalar field. load_path is a VTI or time-series directory. arrayName is\n    the point-data array; it may be empty only when the file has one array.\n\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:4:This guide describes the current scripting API in TopoPilot2Library. The canonical\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:7:    TopoPilot2Library/src/topopilot/ui/script_template.py\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:17:The template already imports ParaView and every public TopoPilot node, validates\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:18:command-line arguments, creates the session/cache directories, renders an image or\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:19:interactive view, and renders time-varying data to frames before invoking ffmpeg.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:25:A TopoPilot pipeline is a directed tree of node objects (or a forest if it has\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:50:exists in the in-memory node tree, TopoPilot reuses its id. Node definitions should\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:91:        # 4. Select the embeddings that the template will render.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:121:    loader. The loaders rename them internally to TopoPilot's standard names.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:122:  * The nodes_to_visualize order is also the rendering order. Put a background\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:154:returns. For video rendering, set any required background properties directly in\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:158:The TopoPilot UI can generate this function from its current ParaView camera. If\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:197:Run it in an environment containing TopoPilot, ParaView, and TTK.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:312:    2-saddles, maxima, and degenerate points, in that order.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:360:    Computes degenerate points of a symmetric tensor field.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:363:    Computes degenerate points of an asymmetric tensor field.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:370:    saddles, maxima, and degenerate points.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:380:    Displays symmetric-tensor degenerate points. The two colors represent\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:381:    trisectors and wedges; radius controls sphere size.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:385:    Displays asymmetric-tensor degenerate points. The two colors represent\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:386:    trisectors and wedges; radius controls sphere size.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:403:    Tracks symmetric-tensor degenerate points over time.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:406:    Tracks asymmetric-tensor degenerate points over time.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:416:    Displays tracked symmetric-tensor degenerate points, colored by identity.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:419:    Displays tracked asymmetric-tensor degenerate points, colored by identity.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:459:  1. Copy script_template.py; do not remove its imports or CLI/rendering body.\n/workspace/TOPOPILOT_SCRIPTING_GUIDE.txt:466:  8. Test one time step to PNG before rendering an entire MP4.\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:2:project(topopilot LANGUAGES CXX)\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:10:# Create the extension module: topopilot/_topopilot_cpp(.so/.pyd)\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:11:pybind11_add_module(_topopilot_cpp cpp/topopilot_backend.cpp)\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:13:target_compile_features(_topopilot_cpp PRIVATE cxx_std_17)\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:15:target_compile_options(_topopilot_cpp PRIVATE\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:21:install(TARGETS _topopilot_cpp\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:22:  LIBRARY DESTINATION topopilot\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:23:  RUNTIME DESTINATION topopilot  # Windows\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/CMakeLists.txt:24:  ARCHIVE DESTINATION topopilot\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:1:# TopoPilot 2\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:5:TopoPilot is installed as a Python library using pip and can be installed in any Python environment that has access to ParaView and the Topology Toolkit. As you may know, ParaView's Python bindings cannot be installed with pip, leading to various headaches.\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:7:We provide instructions to install TopoPilot using Anaconda as well as Python virtual environments. The Anaconda version has no prerequisites (other than Anaconda) and is generally bette behaved. The Python virtual environment version requires that you already have ParaView and TTK installed on your machine, while the Anaconda version does not. It also will be more annoying to work with (see below)\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:18:```pip3 install -e TopoPilot2```\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:37:Finally, install TopoPilot by running\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:39:```pip3 install -e path/to/TopoPilot2```\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:78:```python3 -m pip install -e path/to/TopoPilot2```\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:90:TopoPilot supports the OpenAI and Anthropic APIs. Select an OpenAI or Claude\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:91:model with `topopilot.config.set_llm(...)`; the verifier can be selected\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:95:from topopilot.api import LLM\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:96:from topopilot.config import set_llm, set_verifier_llm\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:118:Set the standard provider environment variable before launching TopoPilot:\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:127:`openai_api_key.txt` or `anthropic_api_key.txt` next to `TopoPilot2Library`:\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:130:TopoPilot2/\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:133:└── TopoPilot2Library/\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:144:```TopoPilot```\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:146:To run just the viewer or just the server, you can instead run ```TopoPilot-Client``` or ```TopoPilot-Server```. If you are only using TopoPilot to perform and save computations, and do not need to visualize anything, then you only need to run the MCP server.\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:150:```/usr/bin/pvbatch: Error while finding module specification for 'topopilot.ui.client' (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'topopilot')```\n/workspace/TopoPilot2Library/README.md:152:If that occurs, then instead of ```TopoPilot```, run ```TopoPilot-NoBatch```, and instead of ```TopoPilot-Client``` run ```Topo